content.text:
Bug/edge case found and fixed in `/tmp/claude-replay-claude_subagent_resume_after_restart/src/math.ts`:

**Issue:** `add` did no runtime validation of its inputs. TypeScript's `strict` mode only guards the call sites it can see at compile time — at runtime, `a` or `b` can still arrive as `NaN` or `Infinity` (e.g. from a failed `parseFloat`, a bad `JSON.parse`, or an upstream calculation gone wrong), and `add` would silently propagate that instead of surfacing the error. For a shared math utility, silent `NaN`/`Infinity` propagation is a classic source of hard-to-trace bugs downstream.

**Fix applied:**
```ts
export const add = (a: number, b: number): number => {
  if (!Number.isFinite(a) || !Number.isFinite(b)) {
    throw new RangeError(`add expects finite numbers, got a=${a}, b=${b}`);
  }
  return a + b;
};
```
This makes invalid input fail loudly at the call site rather than quietly corrupting downstream calculations. There are currently no callers of `add` in the repo (confirmed via grep), so this is a safe, non-breaking change. I also added the explicit `: number` return type annotation for clarity.

